                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]Played On: [b]HTC Vive[/b][/h1]\n\nGuns'n'Stories: Bulletproof VR initially launched in Early Access. After several grueling months of waiting for the fully finished product, the game launched officially on March 8, 2018. Though one could say this is \"another VR wave-shooter\", that classification would be wrong. It is a wave-shooter, but it comes with a solid story that's split up into three acts, each having three chapters that take place in different environments, and one major boss at the end of each act.\n\nYou play as Grandpa, a man who was once a legendary rootin' tootin' cowboy in the west. But if you know anything about Grandpa's, they sometimes tend to exaggerate their stories just a little bit. That's where your grandson, Robbie, comes in; although he hangs on your every word about your weird wild west days, he's not afraid to question your fanatical tales when they get a little too far fetched. As Grandpa, you will battle through hordes of cowboys while being equipped with a giant arsenal of weapons. Your goal is to rescue your beloved Charlotte, all the while spouting off humorous dialogue that ranges from cynical to black. There's even a good dose of pop culture references thrown into the mix. Lok'Tar Ogar!!!\n\nThe graphics and gameplay in this release are downright fantastic. The graphics are some of the cleanest and well produced of any VR game on the market right now. The same can be said for the gun play; it's precise and has a gratuitous amount of haptic feedback to present a realistic, tactile feel. There are all kinds of guns to play around with, which are presented to you automatically in the story mode; dual revolvers, tommyguns, a two-handed flak cannon, and even more unique weaponry such as the Tesla Freeze gun. More can even be unlocked along the way, for use in the bonus stages. There are also several difficulty modes to try out, and even Normal can be brutally hard from the second act onward.\n\nAs you're mowing down enemies, your primary mode of defense is to bat or shoot away the bullets that come toward you using your guns. While this is a unique way of dealing with projectiles, including sticks of dynamite and rockets, some work could be done on the dodging portion. I found myself giving up trying to dodge bullets as Grandpa's hitbox seems to be massive. What I couldn't get around to with my guns, I just soaked up and stayed in a state of near-death for some of the levels. There also seems to be a problem with the Tesla Freeze gun being able to block/bat bullets, as they all went straight through every part of the gun.\n\nWhen I first picked up Guns'n'Stories: Bulletproof VR, the third and final act had not yet been completed. I was delighted when I started playing it again to see that it had finally been added to the game, after what felt like an eternity of waiting. Sadly, this last act feels rushed in many ways. In the very last boss battle, there are lines of dialogue that keep repeating over and over again, and they become seriously stale after a while. This doesn't happen in any other portion of the game. It should also be noted that there is a distinct sound quality difference in the voice clips of the final boss, and it comes off very granular. On the upside, the bonus levels are a lot of fun. It's more wave-based shooting, but at a full 360 degrees rather than the standard 180 from the story. There's also an arcade mode for each level as well.\n\nThe developer is still active in the forums, and is still issuing needed updates, so it's a project that's very well looked after. It's a refreshing change of pace from the plethora of virtual reality titles that are dumped onto Steam without a care. The first chapter of act one can be experienced for free in Guns'n'Stories Preface. I suggest giving that a whirl before putting down the cash on the full release. That being said, Guns'n'Stories: Bulletproof VR is unquestionably one of the greatest wave-shooters for VR. With the amount of guns that you get, environments that you explore, the hilarious dialogue, and an actual committed story, there's not much here to dislike. Grab it at full price, don't let this gem pass you by.\n\n[h1]Rating: [b]4.5/5.0[/b] - Excellent, highly worth playing.[/h1]\nThe VR Network [url=https://store.steampowered.com/curator/33182845-The-VR-Network/]Curator[/url] | [url=https://steamcommunity.com/groups/thevrnetwork]Group[/url] Click for More",
